+
+## Local network and loopback resources
+
+The app permits same-origin scripts to request local network and loopback access.
+Use `fetchLocalResource` from `src/utils/localNetwork.ts` in browser code, from an
+explicit user action after explaining which device or service will be contacted:
+
+```ts
+import { fetchLocalResource } from './utils/localNetwork';
+
+connectButton.addEventListener('click', async () => {
+  try {
+    const response = await fetchLocalResource('http://localhost:45678/health', 'loopback');
+    statusElement.textContent = await response.text();
+  } catch (error) {
+    statusElement.textContent = error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Connection failed.';
+  }
+});
+```
+
+Use `'local'` for LAN devices (including private IPs and `.local` names), and
+`'loopback'` for services on the user's own machine (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, or
+`[::1]`). The helper declares the target address space for HTTP requests from
+HTTPS pages, queries granular permissions when supported, and reports denied
+permissions or connection errors. Unsupported permission queries fall back to a
+normal connection attempt; browser security restrictions still apply.
+
+Deploy the app over HTTPS. Users must grant browser permission when prompted,
+and the target service must allow the app's origin through standard CORS headers.
+Requests run in the user's browser, so loopback refers to their machine. No
+connection is attempted automatically on page load.
